Dear all!

Please excuse my long delay in posting this summary, but my RAID box was=20
temporarily unavailable.

Thanks to:
Allan Rollow, Udo de Boer, Iain Barker, Bob Harris, Dr Thomas Blinn, John=
=20
Lanier.

I received the follwing information/suggestions:

- - The disk size limit was lifted from 1TB to 2TB "rather recently", som=
e
  tools may not yet have been update, e.g., diskconfig, leading it to
  display negative sizes for disks > 1TB.

  Since my RAID system is my backup system, I want to be sure it works,
  so I split the 1.2TB "disks" on my RAID box into 600GB partitions and
  export each as a separate SCSI disk device.  Configuring these with
  diskconfig was no problem.

- - Device names can be reused to avoid gaps in the disk numbering scheme=
,
  e.g. using "dsfmgr -e newname oldname".  The Tru64 Hardware Management
  manual, Sept. 2002, discussed name changes and renumbring in sections
  1.5.3.5 and 1.5.3.6.  Most of those who replied suggested to refrain
  from device name fiddling, if avoidable.
 =20
  The four virtual SCSI disks exported by my RAID box were now placed nic=
ely
  as dsk10 to dsk13, so I decided not to renumber.

- - I noticed some interesting behavior when trying to register the new S=
CSI
  devices with "hwmgr scan scsi".  Three out of four devices were immedia=
tely
  recognized as disks and pertaining disk special files created.  The fou=
rth=20
  was recognized, but flagged with "inconsistencies".  I had to delete th=
is
  device with "hwmgr delete scsi -did ...", and then run "hwmgr scan scsi=
"
  again.  Then also the fourth device was recognized properly.

  For completeness: I am running V5.1B on a GS1280, with a KZPEA two-chan=
nel   =20
  SCSI controller.  The RAID box is an Infortrend EonStor A16U with two
  SCSI channels; two virtual disks are exported on each channel.

Thanks for your help!
Hans
